#!/usr/bin/with-contenv sh
# s6-overlay v3 finish script. Runs when the water_monitor service exits.
#
# s6 calls this with two arguments:
#   $1  the service's exit code, or 256 if it was killed by a signal
#   $2  the signal number, when $1 is 256
#
# WHY THIS INSPECTS $1. It used to call `halt` unconditionally, so EVERY exit
# tore the whole container down — including a normal shutdown, and including a
# transient crash. On a box whose job is closing the water main, a one-off
# uvicorn fault became a dead valve controller until somebody noticed.
#
# The two cases have to be told apart:
#
#   * exit 0, or 256 (a signal, which is how a Supervisor-requested stop
#     arrives) — this is a NORMAL stop. Do not halt: s6 is already running the
#     shutdown it started, and halting again cuts across it. This is the same
#     class of mistake as the v2 `s6-svscanctl -t` call documented below, which
#     tore the supervision tree down underneath services that were still
#     stopping.
#
#   * anything else — a real crash. Halt, so the container exits and the
#     Supervisor sees a stopped add-on. `watchdog:` in config.yaml then brings
#     it back, which is the restart path the repo previously did not have.
#
# HISTORY, kept because it is why the shebang and the halt path look like this.
# This was `s6-svscanctl -t /var/run/s6/services`, the v2 idiom. v3 moved the
# scandir, so that call failed with "No such file or directory" on every
# shutdown - noisy, but inert: it never actually did anything. Repointing it at
# the v3 scandir (/run/service) was worse, because then it WORKED: it tore the
# supervision tree down underneath the services still stopping, and
# legacy-cont-init failed with "command exited 111".
#
# v3 documents halt as the way to bring the container down from a finish
# script, precisely so the shutdown sequence is not disrupted.
#
# `sh` rather than execlineb or bashio, matching the sibling `run` script:
# execline conditionals are easy to get subtly wrong in a file that can only
# fail in production, and bashio needs a different shebang for no gain here.

if [ "${1}" = "0" ] || [ "${1}" = "256" ]; then
    echo "[finish] water_monitor stopped normally (code ${1}, signal ${2:-none});"
    echo "[finish] leaving the shutdown to s6."
    exit 0
fi

echo "[finish] water_monitor exited abnormally (code ${1}, signal ${2:-none})."
echo "[finish] halting the container so the Supervisor watchdog restarts it."
exec /run/s6/basedir/bin/halt
